🚨 **SUGGESTION: harden `onData` with the same `body.isClosed` guard `onError` has**

At `provider_common.dart:780`:

```dart
rawSub = response.stream.listen(
  (data) {
    if (!abandoned) body.add(data);
  },
  onError: (Object error, StackTrace stackTrace) {
    if (abandoned || (cancelToken?.isCancelled ?? false) || body.isClosed) {
      return; // the link's death rattle after abandonment — noise.
    }
    body.addError(error, stackTrace);
  },
  onDone: body.close,
);
```

`onError` checks `body.isClosed` before `addError`, but `onData` only checks `!abandoned`. If a misbehaving source ever delivered a data event after `onDone` ran `body.close()`, `body.add(data)` throws a synchronous `StateError` **inside the raw subscription's data handler** — an unhandled zone error of exactly the class this PR eliminates (`fa crashed` with no harness frames). The Dart SDK's own `_StreamIterator` even carries comments about "really misbehaving streams" emitting after cancel/done.

`package:http` response streams are well-behaved today, so this is hardening, not a bug — but it is one condition for symmetry:

```dart
(data) {
  if (!abandoned && !body.isClosed) body.add(data);
},
```

